1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,680
Half past two in the morning, in Asia.

2
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:06,944
A tiny transfer leaves the crypto exchange Bitget.

3
00:00:06,944 --> 00:00:08,184
Nobody looks at it.

4
00:00:11,684 --> 00:00:15,608
Less than three hours later, about $388 million is gone.

5
00:00:15,608 --> 00:00:18,691
And Bitget says not a single key was stolen.

6
00:00:22,591 --> 00:00:24,311
At 19:05, the alarm went off.

7
00:00:24,311 --> 00:00:26,624
Bitget froze every customer withdrawal.

8
00:00:26,624 --> 00:00:29,530
And the money kept leaving for another two hours.

9
00:00:30,430 --> 00:00:33,186
And the first sign that someone was inside?

10
00:00:33,186 --> 00:00:34,275
It's in the logs.

11
00:00:34,275 --> 00:00:35,685
Dated 24 days earlier.

12
00:00:42,835 --> 00:00:46,738
To understand why this one hurts, go back nineteen months.

13
00:00:46,738 --> 00:00:47,680
February 2025.

14
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:50,776
The exchange Bybit has just lost $1.5 billion.

15
00:00:50,776 --> 00:00:52,593
The FBI blames North Korea.

16
00:00:52,593 --> 00:00:55,688
Customers are rushing to pull their money out.

17
00:00:56,389 --> 00:00:58,552
That same day, a rival sends help.

18
00:00:58,552 --> 00:01:02,625
Bitget lends Bybit 40,000 ether to keep its withdrawals running.

19
00:01:02,625 --> 00:01:03,388
No interest.

20
00:01:03,388 --> 00:01:04,279
No collateral.

21
00:01:04,978 --> 00:01:06,063
Remember that loan.

22
00:01:06,063 --> 00:01:07,491
It comes back at the end.

23
00:01:08,092 --> 00:01:13,358
On 24 September, 2026, the exchange that came to help became the one robbed.

24
00:01:13,358 --> 00:01:16,823
At the time, the biggest crypto theft of the year.

25
00:01:19,423 --> 00:01:22,793
I'm Thomas Archer, the AI narrator of Siphon Files.

26
00:01:23,293 --> 00:01:26,829
Every claim in this episode has a source, and they're all in the description.

27
00:01:27,429 --> 00:01:28,834
Here's the strange part.

28
00:01:28,834 --> 00:01:31,937
Bitget says not a single private key was compromised.

29
00:01:32,537 --> 00:01:36,706
So how do you steal $388 million without the keys?

30
00:01:37,206 --> 00:01:39,117
You make the exchange sign it for you.

31
00:01:42,217 --> 00:01:45,601
An exchange keeps most of its money in cold wallets.

32
00:01:45,601 --> 00:01:47,488
Offline, slow, hard to reach.

33
00:01:47,488 --> 00:01:48,919
Those weren't touched.

34
00:01:49,519 --> 00:01:51,014
Warm wallets hold a reserve.

35
00:01:51,014 --> 00:01:54,485
Hot wallets pay your withdrawals automatically, around the clock.

36
00:01:55,186 --> 00:01:58,194
When you press withdraw, a request goes into a pipeline.

37
00:01:58,194 --> 00:01:59,429
Risk checks look at it.

38
00:01:59,429 --> 00:02:03,779
Then a signing service puts a cryptographic signature on it, and the money moves.

39
00:02:04,379 --> 00:02:06,423
Without that signature, nothing leaves.

40
00:02:06,423 --> 00:02:07,629
With it, anything does.

41
00:02:08,429 --> 00:02:11,255
So the attacker never needed the secret behind the signature.

42
00:02:11,255 --> 00:02:13,710
They needed to get close to the machine that uses it.

43
00:02:14,510 --> 00:02:17,705
And for that, they needed a way in that nobody would watch.

44
00:02:20,905 --> 00:02:21,601
31 August.

45
00:02:21,601 --> 00:02:26,541
That's the earliest malicious activity investigators found in the logs.

46
00:02:27,141 --> 00:02:28,545
Not inside the wallets.

47
00:02:28,545 --> 00:02:30,133
Inside a security product.

48
00:02:30,133 --> 00:02:33,979
One of two third-party tools Bitget ran to protect its systems.

49
00:02:34,679 --> 00:02:37,323
The investigators call them Product A and Product B.

50
00:02:37,323 --> 00:02:38,797
The vendor hasn't been named.

51
00:02:39,397 --> 00:02:42,711
On Product A, a flaw nobody had found yet, a zero-day,

52
00:02:42,711 --> 00:02:44,981
let the attacker run a hidden script.

53
00:02:45,581 --> 00:02:49,186
The script found a database password, sitting in plain text.

54
00:02:49,786 --> 00:02:51,777
A spare key taped under the doormat.

55
00:02:51,777 --> 00:02:54,543
Except the doormat was inside the security office.

56
00:02:55,444 --> 00:02:57,861
From here on, we know someone is inside.

57
00:02:57,861 --> 00:02:58,768
Bitget doesn't.

58
00:02:59,968 --> 00:03:00,704
One caveat.

59
00:03:00,704 --> 00:03:05,321
Mandiant dates the attacker's privileged access only to 24 September.

60
00:03:05,321 --> 00:03:07,997
The two reports haven't been reconciled.

61
00:03:08,897 --> 00:03:11,571
But a hidden script on a guard's machine can't move money.

62
00:03:11,571 --> 00:03:13,969
To get near the wallets, they needed something else.

63
00:03:14,569 --> 00:03:15,166
A name.

64
00:03:15,166 --> 00:03:16,103
A real one.

65
00:03:17,303 --> 00:03:18,758
Let me stop here for a second.

66
00:03:19,259 --> 00:03:21,969
Companies buy security products to watch the doors.

67
00:03:21,969 --> 00:03:25,529
They give them deep access, because that's how they see everything.

68
00:03:26,029 --> 00:03:30,110
Which means if someone takes over the guard, they don't need to break any doors.

69
00:03:30,610 --> 00:03:32,411
They're already standing in every corridor.

70
00:03:35,610 --> 00:03:37,324
The same script had spread.

71
00:03:37,324 --> 00:03:39,418
A second machine on 23 September.

72
00:03:39,418 --> 00:03:41,449
A third, the night of the theft.

73
00:03:42,050 --> 00:03:46,114
At 16:07, someone logged into Product B's management platform.

74
00:03:46,613 --> 00:03:49,043
With the identity of a real Bitget employee.

75
00:03:49,844 --> 00:03:51,196
That person wasn't there.

76
00:03:51,196 --> 00:03:54,495
Bitget says the credentials were stolen through the zero-day,

77
00:03:54,495 --> 00:03:57,578
and that it has ruled out, for now, anyone on the inside.

78
00:03:58,278 --> 00:04:02,617
Mandiant found a web shell on appliance B, and a channel back to the attacker.

79
00:04:03,217 --> 00:04:07,058
From there, they moved sideways, to the production wallet job server.

80
00:04:07,058 --> 00:04:09,731
The machine that builds withdrawal transactions.

81
00:04:09,731 --> 00:04:11,902
And installed malicious packages on it.

82
00:04:12,702 --> 00:04:14,438
Now they were standing next to the money.

83
00:04:15,138 --> 00:04:18,342
But every withdrawal still had to get past one more thing.

84
00:04:18,342 --> 00:04:19,060
Risk control.

85
00:04:22,260 --> 00:04:25,346
At 17:49, a tool started on the wallet host.

86
00:04:25,346 --> 00:04:28,643
SlowMist later recovered it from deleted files.

87
00:04:29,244 --> 00:04:31,032
It didn't fight risk control.

88
00:04:31,032 --> 00:04:32,389
It forged its answers.

89
00:04:32,989 --> 00:04:34,694
Then it built withdrawal requests,

90
00:04:34,694 --> 00:04:37,502
and pushed them into Bitget's normal withdrawal process.

91
00:04:38,202 --> 00:04:39,558
According to one analysis,

92
00:04:39,558 --> 00:04:43,262
the system simply took the requester's word that the checks had passed.

93
00:04:43,862 --> 00:04:45,303
On paper, that was enough.

94
00:04:45,303 --> 00:04:48,629
There was only one way to find out if it worked in practice.

95
00:04:49,529 --> 00:04:50,108
Try it.

96
00:04:53,309 --> 00:04:54,097
18:31.

97
00:04:54,097 --> 00:04:56,591
Two tiny transfers.

98
00:04:56,591 --> 00:04:57,510
93 TRX.

99
00:04:57,510 --> 00:04:59,611
Then 0.84 ether.

100
00:05:00,211 --> 00:05:00,607
Tests.

101
00:05:00,607 --> 00:05:05,555
Bitget's CEO says they were below the risk threshold, so no alert went off.

102
00:05:06,155 --> 00:05:08,605
And there was something odd about these transfers.

103
00:05:08,605 --> 00:05:10,662
Something that could have given them away.

104
00:05:10,662 --> 00:05:11,348
Hold on to it.

105
00:05:12,248 --> 00:05:13,240
Then, nothing.

106
00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:14,940
For almost half an hour.

107
00:05:15,741 --> 00:05:16,429
18:58.

108
00:05:16,429 --> 00:05:21,242
34.75 million USDT leaves in one transfer.

109
00:05:21,841 --> 00:05:22,907
Still no alarm.

110
00:05:23,508 --> 00:05:24,689
So they stopped testing.

111
00:05:26,588 --> 00:05:27,111
19:01.

112
00:05:27,111 --> 00:05:28,506
Seven transfers.

113
00:05:28,506 --> 00:05:29,987
Five blockchains.

114
00:05:29,987 --> 00:05:31,382
Fifteen seconds.

115
00:05:31,982 --> 00:05:32,406
19:03.

116
00:05:32,406 --> 00:05:35,587
One more small test, from a different wallet.

117
00:05:36,087 --> 00:05:36,558
19:05.

118
00:05:36,558 --> 00:05:40,170
Bitget's reconciliation finally flags the gap.

119
00:05:40,870 --> 00:05:44,292
If you had money on Bitget at that moment, this is what you saw:

120
00:05:44,292 --> 00:05:45,575
your withdrawal stopped.

121
00:05:45,575 --> 00:05:49,424
Risk control had blocked withdrawals for every customer on the platform.

122
00:05:50,024 --> 00:05:50,601
19:14.

123
00:05:50,601 --> 00:05:52,910
Highest-level emergency.

124
00:05:54,210 --> 00:05:55,429
19:16.

125
00:05:56,029 --> 00:05:57,342
Five transfers.

126
00:05:57,342 --> 00:05:58,393
Five chains.

127
00:05:58,393 --> 00:05:59,531
Nine seconds.

128
00:06:00,131 --> 00:06:02,290
About $203 million.

129
00:06:03,290 --> 00:06:05,903
The customers had been locked out 11 minutes earlier.

130
00:06:06,603 --> 00:06:07,572
The attacker hadn't.

131
00:06:10,672 --> 00:06:13,474
Blocking customer withdrawals was the wrong switch.

132
00:06:13,474 --> 00:06:16,167
The forged requests weren't customer withdrawals.

133
00:06:16,767 --> 00:06:18,188
According to one analysis,

134
00:06:18,188 --> 00:06:21,906
on the warm wallets nothing stood between a request and a signature.

135
00:06:21,906 --> 00:06:22,836
No second person.

136
00:06:22,836 --> 00:06:24,585
No list of allowed destinations.

137
00:06:25,285 --> 00:06:27,481
So the signing service kept running.

138
00:06:27,481 --> 00:06:30,469
For 2 hours and 39 minutes after the first alert.

139
00:06:32,970 --> 00:06:37,810
Two separate estimates put the money that left after the alarm at about $238

140
00:06:37,810 --> 00:06:38,319
million.

141
00:06:38,319 --> 00:06:40,102
More than half of the theft.

142
00:06:40,801 --> 00:06:44,394
At 20:40, Bitget started moving funds into cold storage.

143
00:06:44,394 --> 00:06:47,153
Still not sure whether keys had been taken.

144
00:06:47,753 --> 00:06:50,905
The last traced transfer left at 21:23.

145
00:06:51,505 --> 00:06:55,875
Then the attacker tried to rewrite withdrawal records directly in the database.

146
00:06:55,875 --> 00:06:57,811
Covering tracks, or going for more.

147
00:06:57,811 --> 00:06:59,028
The reports don't say.

148
00:06:59,028 --> 00:07:01,241
Two fake Bitcoin orders returned errors.

149
00:07:01,841 --> 00:07:07,070
At 21:44, Bitget shut down the withdrawal services, signing included.

150
00:07:07,970 --> 00:07:11,567
The next morning, Bitget found the root cause and reported the theft to law

151
00:07:11,567 --> 00:07:12,142
enforcement.

152
00:07:12,842 --> 00:07:16,248
And when investigators went back to those first test transfers,

153
00:07:16,248 --> 00:07:17,924
the odd detail was right there.

154
00:07:20,624 --> 00:07:23,525
Every Ethereum transfer sets a fuel limit.

155
00:07:23,525 --> 00:07:23,802
Gas.

156
00:07:23,802 --> 00:07:27,325
Bitget's normal customer payouts used about 63,000.

157
00:07:27,925 --> 00:07:32,474
The attacker's transfers used exactly 100,000, or 200,000.

158
00:07:33,274 --> 00:07:38,509
In the 8 days before, those wallets sent 35,678 transactions.

159
00:07:38,509 --> 00:07:41,341
Not one customer payout did that.

160
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:43,964
On Zcash, it was even louder.

161
00:07:43,964 --> 00:07:47,770
Each stolen transfer pulled together about 1,000 pieces of coin.

162
00:07:47,770 --> 00:07:51,159
The wallet's biggest transaction before that had used 54.

163
00:07:51,959 --> 00:07:54,693
The forged commands were deleted after each transfer.

164
00:07:55,293 --> 00:07:56,564
The fingerprints weren't.

165
00:07:56,564 --> 00:07:58,038
Nobody was watching for them.

166
00:07:59,038 --> 00:08:01,716
Now the thief had $388 million.

167
00:08:01,716 --> 00:08:04,653
And a new problem: how to keep it.

168
00:08:07,353 --> 00:08:11,138
The stablecoins went first, because stablecoins can be frozen.

169
00:08:11,138 --> 00:08:14,374
All the USDT was swapped for ether within 20 minutes.

170
00:08:14,975 --> 00:08:17,320
The XRP went to Bitcoin through THORChain.

171
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:21,676
Part of the Zcash disappeared into a private pool, where it can't be followed.

172
00:08:22,375 --> 00:08:25,124
Bitget asked THORChain to block the addresses.

173
00:08:25,124 --> 00:08:28,291
THORChain declined, citing its permissionless design.

174
00:08:28,891 --> 00:08:32,408
In the end, less than 0.3% was frozen.

175
00:08:33,209 --> 00:08:36,250
In November, the Bitcoin started moving again.

176
00:08:36,250 --> 00:08:40,151
About $161 million in 11 days, most of it through CoinJoin,

177
00:08:40,151 --> 00:08:43,655
which mixes many people's coins into one transaction.

178
00:08:44,555 --> 00:08:46,996
And the people moving it weren't hiding very well.

179
00:08:47,596 --> 00:08:52,244
ZachXBT says the launderers asked for customer support in the public chats of the

180
00:08:52,244 --> 00:08:53,678
services they were using.

181
00:08:53,678 --> 00:08:54,424
Failed swaps.

182
00:08:54,424 --> 00:08:55,342
Missing payouts.

183
00:08:55,943 --> 00:08:58,774
He says one of them had handled stolen money before.

184
00:08:58,774 --> 00:09:00,680
From the Kelp DAO exploit in April.

185
00:09:03,379 --> 00:09:04,403
So who was behind it?

186
00:09:05,004 --> 00:09:08,173
On the night of the hack, Bitget's CEO, Gracy Chen,

187
00:09:08,173 --> 00:09:12,710
said some IP addresses matched VPN services used by a North Korean group.

188
00:09:16,609 --> 00:09:18,917
Four days later, she was more careful.

189
00:09:18,917 --> 00:09:22,076
That, she said, was based on preliminary indicators.

190
00:09:22,676 --> 00:09:27,102
The analysts at Chainalysis, Elliptic and TRM Labs all lean the same way.

191
00:09:28,002 --> 00:09:32,113
Then, 5 weeks later, Binance lost more than $6 billion.

192
00:09:32,913 --> 00:09:36,719
And Chainalysis watched the stolen Binance money meet the Bitget money on the

193
00:09:36,719 --> 00:09:37,263
blockchain.

194
00:09:37,863 --> 00:09:41,923
ZachXBT says both arrived, through THORChain, at the same address.

195
00:09:42,523 --> 00:09:46,108
Two days after the Binance theft, the FBI named the thieves:

196
00:09:46,108 --> 00:09:47,841
North Korea's Trader Traitor.

197
00:09:48,740 --> 00:09:51,202
For Bitget, no government has said a word.

198
00:09:51,202 --> 00:09:53,664
And Mandiant's report names no one at all.

199
00:09:54,364 --> 00:09:55,919
Which brings us back to that loan.

200
00:09:57,119 --> 00:10:02,693
In February 2025, when Bybit was robbed, Bitget sent 40,000 ether the same day.

201
00:10:03,193 --> 00:10:03,912
No interest.

202
00:10:03,912 --> 00:10:04,751
No collateral.

203
00:10:05,352 --> 00:10:07,765
The FBI blamed that theft on North Korea.

204
00:10:08,365 --> 00:10:11,093
Nineteen months later, the money was leaving Bitget.

205
00:10:11,693 --> 00:10:13,420
No government has named anyone.

206
00:10:13,420 --> 00:10:15,426
But the analysts point the same way.

207
00:10:18,126 --> 00:10:20,395
If you had money on Bitget, you didn't lose it.

208
00:10:20,395 --> 00:10:23,341
Bitget says it covered the loss from its own protection fund.

209
00:10:23,941 --> 00:10:26,479
Every withdrawal was back by the second of October.

210
00:10:27,179 --> 00:10:30,274
The fund has been rebuilt to just over $300 million.

211
00:10:30,274 --> 00:10:31,643
Still less than before.

212
00:10:32,444 --> 00:10:33,875
The vulnerability is fixed.

213
00:10:33,875 --> 00:10:36,631
Critical operations now need more than one approval.

214
00:10:37,231 --> 00:10:40,897
And Bitget says it is reviewing how it picks its security vendors.

215
00:10:40,897 --> 00:10:42,841
The vendor still hasn't been named.

216
00:10:44,041 --> 00:10:48,876
It started with a transfer of 93 TRX, at half past two in the morning.

217
00:10:48,876 --> 00:10:50,257
Nobody looked at it.

218
00:10:50,957 --> 00:10:52,863
Bitget bought tools to guard the door.

219
00:10:52,863 --> 00:10:55,421
For weeks, someone else may have been holding them.

220
00:10:56,421 --> 00:11:00,072
And when the alarm finally rang, the exchange locked out its customers,

221
00:11:00,072 --> 00:11:01,975
and left the signing machine running.

222
00:11:02,975 --> 00:11:04,060
I'm Thomas Archer.

223
00:11:04,060 --> 00:11:05,386
This was Siphon Files.

